# API key permissions for AID apps

Loop and AAPS upload to Nocturne with an API key. This page lists the permissions to give that key.
A key with too few permissions fails some uploads. A key with too many can do more than the app needs.

To connect an app that signs in with OAuth, see [Connecting your app](/docs/connecting-apps).

## Create the key

1. Open **Settings**, then **Connectors & apps**.
2. In the **API tokens** card, select **Create token**.
   If the page shows an **API information** card, its **Create API key** button opens the same dialog.
3. Enter a label that names the app and the phone, for example `Loop on my phone`.
4. Set the permissions as the section for your app says.
5. Leave **Only last 24 hours** off. That option limits what the key can read to the last 24 hours.
6. Select **Create token**, and copy the token. Nocturne shows it one time.

Enter the token in the app, in the field for the API secret or the access token.
Nocturne accepts the same token in both places.

The **Create API key** button fills in **All health data** with **Read & write**.
That is more than Loop or AAPS needs.
To narrow it, set **All health data** to **No access**.
Then set the rows that your app needs.

A key you moved from Nightscout keeps the access it had there.
The Nightscout API secret becomes a key with **All health data** set to **Read & write**.
That is more than Loop or AAPS needs.
Make a new key for each app with the permissions on this page, then revoke the old key.

## Loop

Set these rows to **Read & write**:

| Permission | What Loop does with it |
| --- | --- |
| Blood glucose | Uploads glucose readings to `/api/v1/entries`. |
| Treatments | Uploads and deletes treatments, such as carbs and overrides, on `/api/v1/treatments`. |
| Device status | Uploads the loop status on `/api/v1/devicestatus`. |
| Treatment profile | Uploads the Loop settings as a profile. |

Leave all other rows at **No access**.

When you add the Nightscout service, Loop calls `/api/v1/experiments/test` to check the key.
That call needs read access to at least one of **Blood glucose**, **Treatments**, or **Device status**.
The permissions above include it.

## AAPS

This section is for AAPS with the **NSClientV3** plugin. That plugin uses the v3 API.
It takes the key as its access token. Set these rows:

| Permission | Level | What AAPS does with it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Blood glucose | Read & write | Uploads and updates entries. |
| Treatments | Read & write | Uploads treatments. Updates them, for example the end of a temporary basal. |
| Device status | Read & write | Uploads device status. |
| Treatment profile | Read & write | Reads and uploads profiles. Updates the `aaps` settings document. |
| Food & meals | Read & write | Syncs the food list and its changes. |

Leave all other rows at **No access**.

AAPS also calls `/api/v3/status` and `/api/v3/lastModified`.
Any valid key can call both.
The last-modified answer lists only the collections that the key can read.

## Trio, iAPS, and xDrip+

Set **All health data** to **Read & write**. The **Create API key** button fills this in.
That covers every area these apps upload.
This page does not yet give a narrower list for these apps.

## All permissions

Do not set **All permissions** on a key for an app.
No upload needs it. It also lets the key delete all records of a data type at once.

## If the key has too few permissions

When the key lacks a permission, Nocturne answers the request with `403 Forbidden`.
The app cannot upload or read that data type.

If the token is wrong, revoked, or expired, Nocturne answers with `401 Unauthorized`.
Loop reports `401` as a wrong secret.

To fix a `403`, make a new key with the missing row.
Nocturne does not change the permissions of an existing key.

<Callout type="info" title="Check what a key can do">

  The **API tokens** card lists the scopes of each key, for example `glucose.readwrite`.
  It shows the time of last use. If there is no time, the app has not reached Nocturne with that key.

</Callout>
